# st.stm32l476.uart_gpio_demo
# Real STM32CubeMX-generated (STM32L476RGTx, Makefile toolchain) LED blink +
# USART2 print - proves the actual generated+compiled firmware boots and
# runs under Renoly, not just the hand-assembled register probe from the
# spike stage. See ../../../../../replica-server/renoly_examples/st/stm32l476/README.md.

using sysbus
mach create "STM32L476_UART_GPIO_Demo"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platform.repl

# Load firmware first so symbols resolve for patching. Bare @$ORIGIN/
# firmware.elf - REQUIRED, not stylistic: server/bundle.py materializes an
# uploaded bundle by literal-string-replacing exactly "$ORIGIN/firmware.elf"
# (see its own docstring) with the real staged path, and every other
# example in this whole tree (F429/F407/NXP) uses this exact bare form -
# this file and freertos_demo's own sim.resc were the only two that instead
# pointed straight at the Makefile build output
# ($ORIGIN/firmware/build/firmware.elf), which doesn't match that
# substitution at all. Over a direct RescParser.parse() (pytest, the CLI
# runner tool) that silently resolved fine against the real nested file, so
# it looked correct - but over the real client -> gRPC StartSession/
# RunSimulation -> materialize_bundle path (what the VS Code extension
# actually uses), the substitution silently no-opped, RescParser then
# failed to find any "firmware/build/firmware.elf" under the temp bundle
# root (which only ever contains a flat firmware.elf), and the CPU faulted
# at boot on unmapped memory - which is why the Timeline stayed completely
# empty for both demos no matter what telemetry-plumbing fix was tried.
# Fixed by keeping a synced flat copy at this demo's own root (sibling to
# this file) - see the "flat firmware.elf copy" note in README.md and the
# auto-sync step in the pytest/CLI tooling that reads this demo.
sysbus LoadELF @$ORIGIN/firmware.elf

# Patch HAL_Delay to return immediately so the demo loop runs at full speed
python "monitor.Parse('sysbus WriteWord 0x%X 0x4770' % (monitor.Machine.SystemBus.GetSymbolAddress('HAL_Delay') & ~1))"
# The emulator MachineTimer drives SysTick; skip CubeMX TIM6 HAL timebase setup.
python "monitor.Parse('sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x%X 0x47702000' % (monitor.Machine.SystemBus.GetSymbolAddress('HAL_InitTick') & ~1))"
# HAL_RCC_Get{Sys,HCLK,PCLK1,PCLK2}Freq are intentionally NOT patched: full RCC
# clock-tree fidelity means the real, unmodified HAL clock math runs against
# STM32L4_RCC's genuinely-stored CR/CFGR/PLLCFGR register state (MSIRANGE,
# SW/SWS, HPRE/PPRE1/PPRE2) and computes the correct 4 MHz MSI-derived
# SYSCLK/HCLK/PCLK1/PCLK2 itself - see stm32l4_rcc.py and README.md.

# USART2_TDR write watchpoint - observes the marker string being transmitted
sysbus AddWatchpointHook 0x40004428 4 Write "print(chr(value & 0xFF), end='')"

# Peripheral watchpoints below use the same "[Tag] Description" convention
# every other example project's sim.resc already uses (CAN_demo_2's own
# I2C1/SPI1 hooks, pwm_demo's SPI5/TIM hooks, etc.) - server/session.py's
# WatchpointHook.label/description pulls the tag straight out of a
# bracketed prefix in the print text, and is_byte_tap (see its own
# _BYTE_TAP_MARKERS) decides whether a hook lands as a Timeline Event or a
# Bus Traffic frame. This untags a real gap: the earlier version of this
# file printed I2C1's own text unbracketed, which still lands as a
# genuine Event (is_byte_tap is false for it) but with an unhelpful
# generic label ("0x40005404"/"register write") instead of reading as
# I2C1 at a glance - and ADC1/DAC1/SPI2 had no watchpoints at all.

# I2C1_CR2 (0x40005404) write watchpoint, firing only when the real
# I2C_GENERATE_START() macro's START bit (bit13=0x2000) is set - direct,
# unambiguous proof the real HAL_I2C_IsDeviceReady() code genuinely issued
# a START during boot (not inferable from ISR's final state alone, since a
# no-op scan and a correctly-detected-and-cleared NACK both leave ISR back
# at 0x1 - see test_stm32l476_initial.py). See stm32l4_i2c.py's docstring.
# Not a byte-tap -> lands as an [I2C1] Event.
sysbus AddWatchpointHook 0x40005404 4 Write "if value & 0x2000: print('[I2C1] CR2 START condition')"

# ADC1 CR (0x50040008) write watchpoint, firing only when the real
# LL_ADC_REG_StartConversion() bit (ADSTART, bit2=0x4) is set - direct
# proof HAL_ADC_Start() genuinely issued a start, not just configured one.
# Not a byte-tap -> lands as an [ADC1] Event.
sysbus AddWatchpointHook 0x50040008 4 Write "if value & 0x4: print('[ADC1] CR ADSTART set - regular conversion started')"

# DAC1 DHR12R1 (0x40007408) write watchpoint - the real 12-bit right-
# aligned code HAL_DAC_SetValue() writes. %03X (3 hex digits, a 12-bit
# value) deliberately avoids is_byte_tap's %02X/X2/%x markers (those are
# for single-byte UART/SPI/I2C taps) -> lands as a [DAC1] Event, not a
# byte-stream bus frame.
sysbus AddWatchpointHook 0x40007408 4 Write "print('[DAC1] DHR12R1=0x%03X' % (value & 0xFFF))"

# SPI2 DR (0x4000380C) write watchpoint - same real idiom as every SPI-
# touching example in this repo (CAN_demo_2's [SPI1 TX], pwm_demo's
# [SPI5 TX]): the exact byte HAL_SPI_TransmitReceive() clocks out. IS a
# byte-tap (%02X matches is_byte_tap's own markers, by design - SPI is a
# byte-stream peripheral like UART) -> lands as Bus Traffic on an "SPI2
# TX" bus lane, not a Timeline Event, matching every other SPI example.
sysbus AddWatchpointHook 0x4000380C 1 Write "print('[SPI2 TX] DR=0x%02X' % (value & 0xFF))"

start
